  "findings": [
    {
      "finding_id": "W1-001",
      "severity": "medium",
      "items": [
        "D-205",
        "D-206",
        "D-203",
        "D-208"
      ],
      "claim": "The 'Request cookies are parsed leniently' decision now percent-decodes request cookie values (cookie-parser style) but keeps D-205's reject-on-write with no matching percent-encode on res.addCookie. Read and write are therefore asymmetric: a value the app legally sets, e.g. setValue(\"a%20b\") or a token containing \"%3D\" (all cookie-octets), comes back from req.cookie(...) as \"a b\" / \"=\", so it no longer equals what was set; and the decision's own echo case res.addCookie(req.cookie(\"x\")) now throws for a perfectly valid incoming cookie \"a%20b\", because the decoded space is outside the grammar. Express avoids this because res.cookie encodes with encodeURIComponent on write; NaruServe's write side cannot encode under D-205 as frozen (it must reject). The T34 round-trip and the echo behaviour would both be surprising to an Express user and to a Servlet user (Servlet does not decode).",
      "evidence": "docs/research/naruserve-v1/waves/wave-5/design.md:114; docs/research/naruserve-v1/waves/wave-5/design.md:49 (D-205); docs/research/naruserve-v1/waves/wave-5/rounds/r2-merged.json (W1-003/W2-004 offered 'keep raw' as an equal option)",
      "suggested_fix": "Make read and write symmetric: either keep request values raw (strip only surrounding DQUOTEs, no percent-decoding, Servlet behaviour) and say so in docs/api/cookie.md, or keep decoding but state that res.addCookie/Cookie serialisation percent-encodes characters outside cookie-octet (validation under D-205 then applies only to the name and to CR/LF/NUL in the value). Add a T34 case that sets a value containing '%XX', reads it back and echoes it."
    }
  ]
